#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180733 :tutorial/content "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification.\n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0$. Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}$.\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{36a^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\mid 6a \\mid &=  \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}6 & \\cdot & a\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\mid a \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0$, and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6 \\mid a \\mid = 6a\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "414812ea-abcc-59df-98fa-8a79ae36a1a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5905 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180736 :tutorial/content "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0$. Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}$.\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{t^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{(t)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\mid t \\mid.& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0$. To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mid t \\mid = -t\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2$. Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{t^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-t &=  \\\\\n- (-2) &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f0207ef-9d6a-584b-8d44-a92c732cce4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5928 :tutorial/title "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Negative Variables"}]}
